title: OT Prophecy: God’s Grace in a Conditional Promise
description: The conditional nature of Ezekiel’s temple vision as God’s grace, an offered chance of national repentance despite His foreknowledge, and why New Testament prophecy takes precedence over the Old Testament’s “glass darkly.”
output: lessons/prophecy/ot-prophecy-conditional.html

# Built from studies/data/notes/ot-prophecy-conditional-notes.html, section by section (1 to 6 and the conclusion).
# The closing paragraph of the notes after "---" is a note about formatting the text, not part of the teaching; it is left off.

=== Introduction
--- OT Prophecy: God’s Grace in a Conditional Promise [title]
overline: Prophecy & the covenants
heading: OT Prophecy: God’s Grace in a Conditional Promise
sub: Ezekiel’s temple vision, the Mosaic Covenant, and why New Testament prophecy takes precedence
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

=== 1. God’s grace
--- God’s Grace in Offering a Conditional Promise
kicker: 1
lead: The conditional nature of Ezekiel’s temple vision ([[Ezekiel 43:10-11]]) reflects God’s grace in extending an opportunity for national repentance, despite His foreknowledge of Israel’s failure.
scripture: Ezekiel 43:10-11 | mark: that they may be ashamed of their iniquities; if they be ashamed of all that they have done
p: This aligns with the Mosaic Covenant at Mount Sinai ([[Exodus 19:5-6]]; [[Deuteronomy 28:1-68]]), which offered blessings for obedience and curses for disobedience, culminating in the call to “choose life” ([[Deuteronomy 30:19]]).

--- God’s Grace Is Evident in Several Ways
kicker: 1
panel: Opportunity Despite Foreknowledge | God knew Israel would “go a whoring after their idols” ([[Ezekiel 6:9]]; [[Hosea 11:7]]), yet He provided a vision of restoration: a temple, priesthood, and kingdom where the Messiah would rule ([[Zechariah 6:12-13]]). This demonstrates His longsuffering and desire for repentance ([[2 Peter 3:9]]), offering Israel a path to blessing even after their covenant-breaking in the wilderness, monarchy, and exile.
panel: Conditional Framework as Grace | The conditionality in [[Ezekiel 43:11]] and [[Zechariah 6:15]] underscores God’s willingness to restore Israel fully, had they repented as a nation. This mirrors His grace in earlier calls to repentance (e.g., [[Jeremiah 3:12-14]]), showing that God’s promises were not revoked arbitrarily but depended on Israel’s response.
panel-warn: Lamentable Loss | The unfulfilled vision is a poignant reminder of what could have been: a literal OT kingdom with Christ as king and priest ([[Zechariah 6:13]]). God’s grace shines through in offering this glorious future, yet Israel’s rejection ([[Hosea 4:6]]) highlights the tragedy of their choice, making the outcome “lamentable” yet consistent with God’s justice.

--- “If ye will diligently obey”
kicker: 1 · The conditions
scripture: Ezekiel 43:11 | mark: if they be ashamed of all that they have done
scripture: Zechariah 6:15 | mark: if ye will diligently obey the voice of the LORD your God
p: This perspective frames OT end-times prophecy as an expression of divine grace, where God extends hope to a rebellious nation, knowing they would likely choose poorly but still providing a pathway to restoration.

--- What Could Have Been
kicker: Ezekiel 40-48 · A conditional promise
image: lessons/prophecy/ot-prophecy-conditional-ezekiel.svg | Ezekiel's temple vision, a conditional promise. Had Israel repented as a nation: a temple, priesthood, and kingdom where the Messiah would rule, what could have been. Israel rejected the conditions: covenant curses, exile, scattering, and desolation. God's grace prepared a new covenant, fulfilled in Christ and the church: Christ the true temple and the church as God's dwelling place. | [[Ezekiel 43:11]] · [[Deuteronomy 28:63-65]] · [[John 2:19-21]] · [[1 Corinthians 3:16]]

=== 2. OT end-times prophecy
--- Implications for OT End-Times Prophecy
kicker: 2
lead: The conditional nature of Ezekiel 40-48 reshapes how we interpret OT end-times prophecy, particularly from a non-dispensationalist, post-tribulation perspective.
panel: Conditionality in OT Prophecy | Many OT prophecies, such as those in Ezekiel, Isaiah (e.g., [[Isaiah 11:1-9]]), and Zechariah, carry implicit or explicit conditions tied to the Mosaic Covenant. For example, [[Zechariah 6:15]] links the Messiah’s temple to obedience, suggesting that prophecies of a restored kingdom were not guaranteed but contingent. This challenges literalist interpretations that expect a future fulfillment without acknowledging Israel’s covenant failure.

--- Curses, Then a Spiritual Fulfillment
kicker: 2
panel-warn: Non-Fulfillment as Fulfillment of Curses | Israel’s failure to meet the conditions (e.g., [[Ezekiel 43:11]]; [[Deuteronomy 30:1-3]]) led to the activation of covenant curses: exile, scattering, and desolation ([[Deuteronomy 28:63-65]]; [[Leviticus 26:33]]). The absence of Ezekiel’s temple in history confirms the covenant’s consequences, not a postponement of prophecy (as dispensationalism might argue). Instead, God’s justice upheld the covenant terms, while His grace prepared a new covenant.
panel: Shift to Spiritual Fulfillment | Because Israel rejected the conditions, OT prophecies like Ezekiel’s temple find their ultimate fulfillment in Christ and the church, not a restored national Israel. For instance, the promise of God’s presence in the temple ([[Ezekiel 43:7]]) is fulfilled in Christ as the true temple ([[John 2:19-21]]) and the church as God’s dwelling place ([[1 Corinthians 3:16]]). This aligns with a post-tribulation view that sees end-times prophecy culminating in Christ’s return, not a separate Jewish dispensation.

=== 3. NT prophecy takes precedence
--- Why NT Prophecy Differs and Takes Precedence
kicker: 3
lead: The NT’s prophetic framework differs from the OT’s “glass darkly” prophecy ([[1 Corinthians 13:12]]) because it reveals God’s ultimate plan through Christ, superseding the conditional and shadowy nature of OT promises.
panel: Clarity of the New Covenant | The OT, seen “through a glass, darkly,” offered prophecies bound to the Mosaic Covenant, which was “ready to vanish away” ([[Hebrews 8:13]]). [[Hebrews 7:18-19]] declares:
scripture: Hebrews 7:18-19
p: NT prophecy, rooted in Christ’s finished work ([[Hebrews 9:26]]), provides a clearer revelation of God’s eternal plan, no longer conditional but guaranteed by Christ’s obedience ([[Romans 5:19]]).

--- Christ as Fulfillment
kicker: 3
p: NT prophecy reinterprets OT visions in light of Christ. For example, Ezekiel’s temple, unbuilt due to Israel’s failure, finds its fulfillment in Jesus, who is both temple and high priest ([[Hebrews 9:11-12]]). The NT emphasizes a spiritual kingdom ([[John 18:36]]) and a future consummation at Christ’s return ([[Revelation 21:1-3]]), not a restored OT system. This aligns with a pre-wrath, post-tribulation view that expects one return of Christ to judge, resurrect, and establish the eternal kingdom, not a separate Jewish millennium.

--- Superseding the Mosaic System
kicker: 3
p: [[Hebrews 10:9]] declares:
big: Hebrews 10:9
p: The Mosaic Covenant’s sacrifices and temple were shadows ([[Hebrews 10:1]]), pointing to Christ’s once-for-all atonement ([[Hebrews 10:10-12]]). OT prophecies like Ezekiel 40-48, tied to that system, are fulfilled in Christ’s redemptive work, not a future literal temple, which would contradict the NT’s emphasis on the finality of His sacrifice.

--- Glass Darkly vs. Face to Face
kicker: 3
p: The OT’s “darkly” prophecies were limited by their context under the law, often veiled in types and shadows ([[Colossians 2:16-17]]). The NT, through the Spirit, reveals the mystery of Christ and the church ([[Ephesians 3:4-6]]), providing a clearer eschatological vision.
parallel: Old Testament · through a glass, darkly | New Testament · a clearer revelation
row: Bound to the Mosaic Covenant, “ready to vanish away” [[Hebrews 8:13]] | Rooted in Christ’s finished work [[Hebrews 9:26]]
row: Conditional and shadowy | No longer conditional but guaranteed by Christ’s obedience [[Romans 5:19]]
row: Veiled in types and shadows [[Colossians 2:16-17]] | The mystery of Christ and the church [[Ephesians 3:4-6]]
row: Ezekiel’s temple, unbuilt due to Israel’s failure | Jesus, both temple and high priest [[Hebrews 9:11-12]]
p: For example, [[Revelation 21:22]] states there is “no temple” in the New Jerusalem, superseding Ezekiel’s vision:
big: Revelation 21:22

=== 4. Choose life today
--- God’s Grace and the Call to Choose Life Today
kicker: 4
lead: The conditional nature of Ezekiel’s vision underscores God’s grace in offering Israel a chance to repent, despite their foreknown rebellion. This grace extends to all humanity through the new covenant.
p: [[Deuteronomy 30:19]]’s call to “choose life” finds its ultimate fulfillment in choosing Christ, who is “the way, the truth, and the life” ([[John 14:6]]). The lamentable failure of Israel serves as a warning:
big: Romans 11:21
p: Yet, God’s grace ensures salvation through faith in Christ, not the works of the law ([[Galatians 3:24-26]]).

=== 5. God’s sovereignty
--- God’s Sovereignty Over Human Choices
kicker: 5
p: God’s foreknowledge and sovereignty ensure that He…
big: Isaiah 46:10 | mark: Declaring the end from the beginning, and from ancient times the things that are not yet done
p: He knows the outcome of all human choices, yet grants free will to choose obedience or rebellion under the Mosaic Covenant. Despite anticipating Israel’s failure ([[Deuteronomy 31:16-18]]), God’s sovereign will remains unthwarted; He works “all things after the counsel of his own will” ([[Ephesians 1:11]]), using even poor choices to serve His purposes.
p: Israel’s rejection of Ezekiel’s temple vision led to covenant curses but opened the way for the new covenant in Christ ([[Hebrews 8:6-8]]), demonstrating that God’s redemptive plan incorporates human free will to fulfill His eternal glory.

=== 6. End-times eschatology
--- Implications for End-Times Eschatology
kicker: 6
lead: From a post-tribulation, pre-wrath perspective, this view impacts how we approach OT end-times prophecy:
panel: Unified Eschatology | Rejecting dispensationalism, this perspective sees one plan of redemption culminating in Christ’s return after the tribulation but before God’s wrath ([[Matthew 24:29-31]]; [[1 Thessalonians 5:9]]). OT prophecies like Ezekiel’s are not postponed to a future Jewish age but fulfilled in Christ and the church, with final consummation in the eternal state.
panel-warn: Warning Against Literalism | Insisting on a literal future temple risks missing the NT’s spiritual fulfillment and may imply a return to the old covenant, which [[Hebrews 8:13]] declares obsolete. Ezekiel’s vision, as a conditional promise, was forfeited, redirecting prophecy to Christ’s universal kingdom.
panel: God’s Faithfulness | Despite Israel’s failure, God’s promises are fulfilled through the new covenant, demonstrating His unchanging character ([[Hebrews 13:8]]). The “what could have been” of Ezekiel’s temple highlights the tragedy of rebellion but magnifies God’s grace in providing a better hope ([[Hebrews 7:19]]).

=== Conclusion
--- Conclusion
p: The conditional interpretation of Ezekiel 40-48 reveals God’s grace in offering Israel a chance for a restored kingdom, despite their foreknown disobedience.
p: Their failure under the Mosaic Covenant explains the non-fulfillment of OT end-times prophecies like the temple vision, while the NT’s clearer revelation, through Christ’s work and the new covenant, takes precedence over the OT’s “glass darkly” promises.
p: This perspective, rooted in a post-tribulation, pre-wrath, non-dispensationalist framework, emphasizes Christ as the fulfillment of all prophecy, urging believers today to “choose life” by faith in Him.
p: The lamentable loss of Ezekiel’s vision underscores the consequences of rejecting God’s grace, yet His mercy prevails in the eternal kingdom ([[Revelation 22:3-5]]).
